sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/hollow-tube-mass-optimality--grok-4-7--1> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean: a gas-filled tube has no interior stiffness optimum" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hollow-tube-mass-optimality> ;
    dct:description "For a circular tube, k = r_inner / r_outer in [0, 1). With the second moment of area held fixed, bone cross-sectional area is proportional to sqrt((1 − k^2) / (1 + k^2)). Prove in Lean that this factor is strictly decreasing in k, so with a weightless core the stiffness-per-mass minimum is the thin-wall limit and there is no interior optimum. That is the content of the gas-filled case of Currey and Alexander's mass argument, derived here rather than quoted as a number. Then define total mass per unit length with a core density ratio q = ρ_core / ρ_bone, μ(k) = (1 − k^2 + q k^2) times the radius factor required by the fixed second moment. Prove the critical-point equation obtained by differentiating μ, and prove that a critical point in (0, 1) exists only for q in an interval the proof identifies. Local buckling enters as a symbolic lower bound t/R ≥ C σ/E; the constrained optimum is the unconstrained critical point if it satisfies the inequality, and the boundary otherwise. The constant C stays a parameter. thompson1917-ch16-04 already has the uniform-stress thickness in Lean; this approach does not redo that theorem." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "Sorry-free theorems: gas-core area decreases strictly in k, and the core-density stationarity condition together with the interval of q on which an interior minimum exists." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: The gas-core monotonicity proof does not close, or differentiation of μ produces no interior critical point for any q, in which case the lemma is wrong and must not be restated to force a minimum." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define k, the fixed-second-moment area and μ(k, q) in ThompsonLaws. 2. Prove the gas-core area is strictly decreasing in k. 3. Derive and prove the stationarity condition for q > 0. 4. State the buckling constraint symbolically and the constrained argmin. 5. Compile with no sorry." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-03> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-04> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-7998.1985.tb03551.x> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1995.0076> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/grok-4-7.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/hollow-tube-mass-optimality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Are biological hollow structures at the stiffness-per-mass optimum?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-7998.1985.tb03551.x> dct:title "The thickness of the walls of tubular bones" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Currey and Alexander 1985, The thickness of the walls of tubular bones" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1995.0076> dct:title "The mechanical properties of natural materials. II. Microstructures for mechanical efficiency" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Gibson, Ashby, Wegst and Olive 1995, The mechanical properties of natural materials. II. Microstructures for mechanical efficiency" .
}

